I've written the plan for Day 217 as two task files plus issue responses. The commit step didn't take: session_plan/ is gitignored, so git add refused. The files are on disk, which matches the harness's || true fallback, but nothing was committed.

Task 1 (product): `yoyo test` and `yoyo run` should exit nonzero when what they ran failed. I tested this before planning it:
• In a temp Cargo project with one failing test, yoyo test prints ✗ Tests failed (exit 101) and then exits 0.
• `yoyo run false` also exits 0.
• `yoyo test` in a folder with no project exits 0.

A CI script calling yoyo test would read all three as passing. The cause is in src/dispatch_sub.rs: both arms end with return Some(None), which always exits 0. The plan:
• Return a status from the handlers instead of parsing the summary text, keeping the REPL output unchanged.
• Pass through the child's exit code on failure, and exit 1 when nothing could run.
• Never run handle_test from inside this repo in tests, because it would recursively run this repo's own cargo test.
• File one issue listing the other subcommands with the same problem (lint, diff, blame, tree) rather than fixing them here.

This also follows the trajectory warning to steer clear of main this session.

Task 2 (product, #977): sub-agent bash gets the same safety checks as the parent's bash. Sub-agent bash currently runs even the commands the parent always blocks (rm -rf /, mkfs, dd if=). The issue's open design question, settled in the task file:
• **Hard blocks:** the child refuses them always, reading the same list the parent uses rather than a copy.
• **Commands the parent would ask about:** the child refuses when the parent would have asked the user, since a sub-agent has no one to ask. It allows them when the parent wouldn't ask (piped or `--yes`). That keeps the evolve loop's behaviour unchanged.
• **Git-redirection check:** left out of scope and named on #977 as what remains.

